All tests pass. Here's the implementation that was added to `lib/utils/hex.c`:

```c
int char2hex(char c, uint8_t *x)
{
	if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {
		*x = c - '0';
	} else if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') {
		*x = c - 'a' + 10;
	} else if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') {
		*x = c - 'A' + 10;
	} else {
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	return 0;
}
```

The function converts a single hex character to its 4-bit numeric value:
- `'0'`–`'9'` → 0–9
- `'a'`–`'f'` → 10–15 (case-insensitive)
- `'A'`–`'F'` → 10–15 (case-insensitive)
- Any other character returns `-EINVAL`

This matches the docstring contract in `include/zephyr/sys/util.h`, follows the same style as the sibling `hex2char` function, and passes all unit test expectations.


